\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Product Signaling Through User Reviews
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
December 3, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first World Wide Web server and browser opened for commerical use in
 1991.
 Thereafter, a number of technological innovations have emerged, including
 the first online shopping system.
 Online shopping is a form of electronic commerce which affords consumers
 the ability to purchase goods and services from a firm over the Internet.
 Today, the Internet is populated by a growing number of U.S.-based online
 retail stores.
 Using figures from a variety of sources, including data from the U.S.
 Census Bureau, the Singapore-based company ReferralCandy determined that
 there are now 102,728 e-commerce retailers in the United States that are
 generating at least $12,000 per year in revenue (Belicove, 2013).
 In fact, the three largest online retailing corporations, Alibaba, Amazon.com,
 and eBay, accumulated over $3 billion dollars of revenue in 2013, while
 e-commerce business-to-consumer product sales totaled $142.5 billion, representi
ng about 8% of retail product sales in the U.S.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The emergence of this hot new industry affords economists the opportunity
 to conduct truly innovative research within the field of industrial organizatio
n.
 Due to the extensive use of game theory in industrial organizaiton, experimenta
l economics has been especially keen on formulating experimental designs
 associated with market structure, firm objectives, industry studies, etc.
 As experimental economics has progressed, so has the complexity of the
 markets studied in the lab.
 Some of the primary areas of interest include: product quality, asymmetric
 information, and market failures.
 Moral hazard is a special case of information asymmetry that is especially
 concerning to economists because it often leads to market failures.
 Note that different kinds of moral hazard can arise with asymmetric information
, and there are various situations when it transpires.
 For instance, moral hazard comes about when the party with more information
 about its intentions has an incentive to behave inappropriately from the
 perspective of the party with less information.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the context of industrial organization, consider the following standard
 example of asymmetric information: sellers know the quality of their products,
 while the quality cannot directly be observed by buyers.
 Furthermore, the costs of sellers and the utility of buyers depend on the
 quality of the product, which is another common occurence.
 The moral hazard that arises in this situation is caused be the inability
 of buyers to perceive the characteristics of sellers.
 One possible solution to the problems of moral hazard involves simple,
 indirect communication from one set of agents to the other.
 Sellers are allowed to invest in costly, observable 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

signals
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that are delivered to the buyers.
 To clarify, the seller observes his own type, which the buyer cannot discern,
 and he chooses to send a signal to the buyer based on his type.
 Once the buyer receives the signal, he decides whether or not to purchase
 from the seller.
 With face-to-face transactions, these signals can include physical advertisemen
ts (billboards, signs, etc.), inspection of merchandise before purchase,
 discussion and bargaining with clerks, etc.
 Economic experiments evaluating asymmetric information often try to determine
 the different equilibria that can arise and under what conditions when
 signaling is allowed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Online businesses, however, have eliminated the need for physical stores,
 so they operate exclusively over the Internet.
 Customers are attracted to online shopping because of convenience, quick
 comparisons, broad product selection, and competitive prices.
 Consumers find a product of interest by either visting the website of a
 retailer directly, or by searching for a group of vendors that sell a certain
 item.
 Though, online shopping still has some notable disadvantages.
 Product delivery and security of personal information are two primary concerns,
 and returning or exchanging an item can be incredibly cumbersome.
 Above all, consumers are at a higher risk of fraud by shopping online.
 To elaborate, buyers are limited in their ability to gather all of the
 relevant information about a good since they are unable to inspect it or
 have face-to-face communication with a seller.
 Consequently, sellers are no longer required to relay any type of signal
 to buyers.
 While sellers can still invest in whatever product quality they see fit,
 they now have the incenive to produce lower quality goods.
 This scenario is a prime example of another type of moral hazard that arises
 with asymmetric information.
 It comes about because buyers are unable to directly observe the actions
 of sellers.
 To sum up, the problems of moral hazard are inherent in most Internet market
 institutions, but product signaling is unable to alleviate the market failures
 associated with these issues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Yet, there still may be a way to overcome the drawback of moral hazard.
 Sellers no longer need to send signals to buyers prior to a transaction.
 Instead, buyers can leave reviews on a product for other consumers to see.
 In fact, many online stores allow customers to comment on and/or rate their
 items.
 One of the great benefits of online shopping is the ability to read product
 reviews, written either by experts or fellow online shoppers.
 Through online user reviews, potential buyers of goods over the Internet
 are able to discern some of the information concerning the quality of a
 product.
 Moreover, these user reviews give customers the option of shopping for
 different purchases from all over the world without having to depend on
 local retailers.
 In addition to online reviews, peer recommendations on online shopping
 pages or social media can also influence future purchases of a good.
 The Neilson Company conducted a survey in March 2010 to determine the impact
 of user reviews on online shopping.
 According to the research, reviews play an important role in influencing
 consumers who tend to make purchases online.
 Most online shoppers indicate that they must first consult online reviews
 before buying an item.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Literature Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Market Failure and Signaling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the most important areas of economic research is concerned with the
 identification of the different causes of market failures.
 As stated previously, the problems of moral hazard that arise with asymmetric
 information often cause market failures.
 So, many economic experiments are structured to allow the possibility of
 market failure in order determine the circumstances and structures needed
 to establish efficient efficient outcomes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Lynch, Miller, Plott, and Porter (1986) conducted sesssions in which sellers
 would first choose two quality levels.
 Both sellers' costs and buyers' values depended on quality, which was unable
 to be directly observed by the buyers.
 Also, the high quality 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

supers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 yielded a greater surplus of value over cost than the lower-quality 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

regulars.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Products were sold in a variation of an oral double auction.
 Forcing sellers to advertise the true quality of the product lead to an
 efficient provision of the high-quality product.
 Yet, removing sellers' identities and providing only post-purchase quality
 information led to inefficient 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

lemons
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 outcomes in which low-quality products were produced and sold at correspondingl
y low prices.
 When sellers' identities were observed, the overall proportion of efficient,
 high-quality units sold increased.
 Efficiency was also increased by giving sellers the opportunity to offer
 a warranty.
 These results suggest that the development of reputations, and remvoing
 some of the risk on the side of buyers, are important factors that could
 lead to efficient outcomes.
 Moreover, Holt and Sherman (1990) observed lemons outcomes in posted-offer
 auctions with a large number of quality 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

grades.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Still, allowing sellers to invest in costly, observable signals could help
 alleviate the problems of moral hazard.
 If the cost of signaling is lower for a high-quality product, then it is
 often the case that there is a separating equilibrium in which the buyers
 can infer the unobserved quality from equilibrium signals.
 Miller and Plott (1985) designed and conducted an experiment with these
 features.
 To elaborate, sellers possessed products that were exogenously designated
 as either grade 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

regular
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or grade 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

super.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Buyers had higher value for products graded as super, but the grade could
 not be observed prior to purchase.
 Sellers could add units of quality, which were also valued by buyers, and
 these added quality units could be observed prior to purchase.
 The cost of adding quality to supers was lower than the cost of adding
 quality to regulars.
 Due to these conditions, the possibility of signaling existed in all of
 the constructed eleven markets.
 The authors attempted to examine several different models and equilibria
 predictions using the experiment.
 According to the results, signaling was a real phenomenon.
 When the marginal cost of signaling was relatively low, quality separation
 occured, and there was no evidence of pooling.
 Quality separation occured infrequently and aspects of pooling were observed
 when the marginal cost of signaling was relatively high.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
User Reviews
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Most of the research concerning user reviews are empirical studies, yet
 few economic papers exist in the literature.
 Instead, the impact of user reviews is often analyzed through the persepectives
 of marketing, management, and business.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One of the major questions researchers wish to answer is: do online reviews
 really matter? Duan, Gu, and Whinston (2008) attempted to answer this very
 question by examining both the persuasive effect and the awareness effect
 of online user reviews on movies' daily box office performance.
 After accounting for endogeneity, their results show that the rating of
 online user reviews has no significant impact on movies' box office revenues.
 This indicates that online user reviews may have little persuasive effect
 on consumer purchase decisions.
 Yet, the value of online posting significantly influence box office sales,
 suggesting the importance of an awareness effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hu, Liu, and Zhang (2008) also studies the effect of online reviews on sales
 by considering both quantitative and qualitative aspects of online reviews.
 The authors adopt a portfolio approach to assess the effectiveness of online
 reviews using transactions cost economics and uncertainty theories.
 The results show that consumers pay attention to both review scores and
 contexual information, such as the reviewer's reputation.
 Also, consumers can distinguish the difference in value between favorable
 and unfavorable reviews, and they respond accordingly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Kim and Gupta (2011) investigated the effects of expression in online word-of-mo
uth (eWOM).
 The authors conducted two experiments to study how consumers interpret
 emotional expressions in line user reviews and the subsequent impact on
 their product evaluations.
 Apparently, negative emotional expressions in a negative review decrease
 the reviews' informative value, and make subsequent product evaluations
 less negative, because consumers believe the reviewer has irrational dispositio
ns.
 On the other hand, postive emotional expression in a positive review do
 not significantly influence consumers' product evaluations even though
 positive emotions are attributed to the product.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Contribution to Existing Literatue
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the descriptions above, one can see that a large amount of the recent
 research on online reviews has focused on measuring the effect user reviews
 has on product sales.
 Even more studies than the ones just mentioned have been dedicated to assessing
 this relationship.
 There have also been papers dedicated to understanding the motivation of
 consumers to post online reviews in general (Hennig-Thurau et al., 2004;
 Dellarocas & Narayan, 2006).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This will be one of the first experiments to analyze the effect of user
 reviews on market outcomes.
 It adds to the existing literature by measuring the impact user reviews
 have on market efficiency.
 Also, it will be one of the first 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

signaling
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 experiments attempting to solve the problems of moral hazard through buyer
 controlled signals, as opposed to signals sent by sellers.
 The goal of the experiment is to assess the impact user reviews have on
 sellers' behavior.
 In the presence of online reviews, will sellers adjust their behavior according
ly? Will sellers even attempt to invest in the quality of their products,
 and how will they adjust their prices? Above all, do user reviews alleviate
 the inefficiencies caused by moral hazard? 
\end_layout

