The low cost dimension refers to the price buyers must pay in order to leave
 a review.
 The treatment variable is whether or not buyers are able to leave a review
 or not.
 Each cell of the table above will consist of two dimensions, denoted as
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 for simplicitly.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 dimension corresponds to a set of 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 market periods, while the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 dimension corresponds to an individual market period run within a given
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 For each cell of the experiment, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=10$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j\geq5$
\end_inset

.
 So, within each set 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, the individual market periods will be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

infinitely repeated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 after five have already been completed, with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\nicefrac{3}{5}$
\end_inset

 of another market period occuring.
 In addition, a different cohort of 30 individuals will be recruited to
 participate for each cell.
 Of the subject who attend, half will be randomly chosen to be the sellers
 of similar goods, while the rest of the individuals will act as buyers
 of these goods.
 Prior to the start of each individual market period 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, buyers and sellers will all be assigned a randomly determined ID number,
 so they can be better identified during trading.
 These ID numbers will change at the start of each new market period 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 in order to maintain seller anonymity and prevent reputation building.
 Thus, no information is carried over from earlier periods to later ones.
 The description below is associated with the controlled portion of the
 experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

At the start of each new 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, before any trading begins, sellers must decide whether they wish to produce
 low quality or high quality products.
 Their decision in product quality will not be directly observed by buyers.
 Furthermore, sellers will decide whether to charge a high price or a low
 price for their goods.
 So, they will have the opportunity to adjust both the level of quality
 and the prices at the start of each new market period 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the units of goods sold by the sellers are identical in all character
istics except the quality and price at which they sell.
 Moving on, all sellers will face the same constant marginal cost schedule
 in regards to the type of quality they have chosen, and the units sold
 by the sellers will be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

made to order.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Hence, sellers will only be responsible to pay for the production of a
 good.
 The marginal cost for a low quality good will be 
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{L}=2$
\end_inset

, and the marginal cost for a high quality good will be 
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{H}=6$
\end_inset

.
 The low asking price will be 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{L}=2$
\end_inset

, and the high asking price will be 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{H}=10$
\end_inset

.
 As usual, the profit that sellers receive for a good will be equal to the
 price of the good minus the marginal cost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A particular trading period 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 will involve buyers and sellers interacting.
 The sellers will post their asking prices, and the buyers choose who to
 engage in a trade.
 Buyers can buy any number of units from a given seller, yet they will still
 face a budget constraint.
 So, buyers will be given the same fixed budget of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

experimental tokens,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which will be replinished for each new trading period 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 Even though buyers will not be obligated to purchase any goods, they will
 receive no additional utility from retaining any of their tokens.
 Moreover, buyers will possess the same constant utility function, and they
 will receive a higher level of utility from higher quality goods.
 The utility buyers receive from a high quality good is 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{H}=14$
\end_inset

, and the utility they receive from a low quality good is 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{L}=4$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, the compensation buyers receive will be based on the difference
 in the utilty they obtain from the good minus the price they pay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, the treatment of this experiment will follow the same basic design
 as described above, with a few distinct differences.
 The treatment will allow buyers the opportunity to leave reviews for other
 buyers to see in subsequent trading periods.
 To elaborate, after all trading has concluded for a given market period
 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, buyers will have the option to leave a review for the different units
 of goods they bought.
 In a review, the buyers can identify the seller they purchased from by
 using the designated ID number, and they will indicate whether they were
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

satisfied
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unsatisfied
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 with their purchase.
 Despite the IDs being randomized for each new 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, all of the reviews that sellers receive within a given set of trading
 periods 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 will be maininted and carried over for each 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 market period.
 The reviews attached to a seller will reset after each new set of market
 periods 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 As stated previously, there will be no cost to leave a review for one cell
 of the experiment, while the other cell will have a relatively low marginal
 cost to do so.
 The fixed marginal cost that buyers must pay in order to leave a review
 will be 
\begin_inset Formula $R=3$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Theoretical Predictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
No Reviews
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine a reasonable Nash equilibrium of this 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-person, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

infinitely repeated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 game, first focus on the simple 2-person, single period scenario.
 An explanation of the extensive form representation is as follows: the
 seller makes the first two moves of the game by choosing quality and price,
 then the buyer decides whether to purchase a single unit from the seller.
 Note that the buyer is able to observe the price of a good, yet he is unable
 to determine its quality.
 By solving this game using backward induction, we can determine that the
 pure-strategy, subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium.
 Before trading begins, suppose the seller is set on always charging a high
 price.
 The subsequent subgame is a simultaneous form game that has one pure strategy
 Nash equilibrium: the sellers will produce low quality goods, while the
 buyers will not buy.
 Next, suppose the seller is set on always charging a low price.
 The subsequent simultaneous form subgame also has one pure strategy Nash
 equilibrium: the seller will produce low quality goods, and the buyers
 will purchase them.
 Thus, the seller will choose to produce low quality goods and sell them
 at low prices, and the buyer will elect to purchase the goods.
 This outcome leads to a mutual payoff of 2 for both agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From this simple example, we can now extend the analysis to the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-person, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

infinitely repeated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 game.
 It is evident that the buyers have only one strategy that is dependent
 on the strategy of the seller.
 If the buyers see a high price, then they will never enter into a trade;
 if the buyers see a low price, then they will purchase a good.
 Due to this strategy function, the sellers now only have two strategies
 to consider.
 The sellers can either choose to sell low quality goods at low prices,
 or they can choose to sell low quality goods at high prices.
 This set of strategies constitute a Nash equilibrium because none of the
 agents have an incentive to deviate.
 To see this, suppose one buyer deviates from the given strategy, while
 the rest of the agents remain fixed.
 If the buyer chooses to purchase a good after seeing a high price, then
 the sellers have an incentive to produce a low quality good, which would
 be detrimental to the buyer.
 If the buyer elects not to purchase a good after seeing a low price, then
 he does not experience any gains from trade, but he good have done so if
 did purchase from a seller.
 So, this individual buyer has no incentive to change the given strategy,
 and thus, none of the buyers do.
 Now, let us consider the case when one seller chooses to deviate, while
 the rest of the agents remain fixed.
 If the seller charges a high price at all, then none of the buyers will
 purchase from him.
 Though, the seller will still choose to produce a low quality good, in
 case one of the buyer makes an error.
 The seller would have been better off producing a low quaility good and
 charging a low price in order to extract some gains from trade.
 Note that the seller will never choose to produce a high quality good at
 a low price because he will lose money as a result.
 So, this individual seller has no incentive to change the given strategy,
 and thus, none of the sellers do.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, the outcome of the infinitely repeated game matches that of the
 stage game.
 This is because buyers are unable to dinstinghish the quality of the goods
 available simply by the price.
 Also, there are so many sellers present in the market that the buyers have
 no way of identifying all of the sellers who participate in fraud.
 Lastly, if a buyer is defrauded by a seller, he has no way of identifying
 this seller in the next period, and he has no way of relaying his experience
 to the other buyers.
 Consequently, the buyers have no way of punishing sellers for devious behavior,
 and the Nash equilibrium will persist in all periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Reviews, No Cost
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As one can tell, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no reviews
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 treatment does not lead to the most efficient outcome.
 Obviously, it would be more beneficial for both parties if high quality
 goods were sold at high prices, as opposed to the inefficient alternative.
 So, is there any solution that would lead to the most efficient outcome?
 With reviews, the buyers are able to induce sellers to produce high quality
 goods, which could then be sold at high prices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The reviews allow buyers to relay information to one another about the types
 of goods being sold by any particular seller.
 This information can then be used by others in later periods as a potential
 signal concerning the type of goods available.
 Let buyers adopt the following grim trigger strategy by using the review
 mechansim: (a) Purchase goods at high prices from sellers with strictly
 positive reviews, while continuing to leave positive reviews for sellers.
 Maintain this strategy as long as the quality of the goods purchased remains
 high; (b) If a seller elects to sell a low quality good at a high price,
 then leave a negative review for all buyers to see in the next trading
 period.
 All buyers will subsequently avoid purchasing goods from any seller who
 has at least one negative review.
 With so many sellers in the market and only a limited budget, the buyers
 are able to adopt this grim trigger strategy without any serious reprecussions
 to their overall utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This particular minmax strategy is an example of one strategy profile that
 is not a Nash equilibrium of the stage game, but it allows buyers a way
 to punish deviators.
 The minimum discount factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 that is required to induce all the sellers to sell high quality goods at
 high prices in every trading period (i.e., subgame) is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
4{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\delta^{t} & \geq & 10\\
\frac{4}{1-\delta} & \geq & 10\\
4 & \geq & 10-10\delta\\
10\delta & \geq & 6\\
\delta & \geq & \frac{3}{5}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

According to the Folk Theorem, the minmax strategy profile with 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\geq\nicefrac{3}{5}$
\end_inset

 is a Nash equilibrium of the infinitely repeated game.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The only question left to answer is whether or not buyer will actually leave
 reviews after purchasing an item.
 Well, online product reviews can be represented as a public good.
 It costs time and effect for individuals to leave a review, but their benefit
 extends to the entire society.
 Economic theory predicts that when people share the obligation to provide
 a public good, it tends to be undersupplied.
 Yet, a fair amount of literature in behavioral and experimental economics
 attepts to explain the reasons for public good contribution, for instance:
 Becker (1974); Bernheim (1986); Andreoni (1989); Andreoni (1990).
 From this research, several motives for contributing to public goods have
 been suggested an evaluated in an extensive set of public goods experiments,
 such as altruism, warm glow, reciprocity, and inequality aversion.
 The motive for giving that is best applied to the online review scenario
 would be warm glow, which implies that individuals care only about their
 own level of contributions and are unresponsive to changes in others' contribut
ions.
 However, the goal of this experiment is not to provide explanations for
 why consumers leave product reviews.
 For more information on public goods experiments, the literature provides
 several insights that applies to the context of online product reviews.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Reviews, Low Cost
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this treatment, there is now a cost of 
\begin_inset Formula $R=3$
\end_inset

 in order for consumers to leave a review.
 This cost represents the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

time and effort
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 people expend to write and post a review on a website.
 As one can see, the cost is high enough such that it offsets the additional
 gains buyers receive from purchasing high quality, high priced items.
 So, buyers no longer have any incentive to leave a review.
 Consequently, the previously described trigger strategy breaks down, and
 the inefficient outcome of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no reviews
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 treatment arises once again.
 In fact, buyers are better off purchasing low quality, low priced goods
 instead of high quality, high priced goods and paying to leave a review.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, is there a way to alleviate the problems caused by 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

costly
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 reviews in order to revert back to the efficient outcome? Well, Amazon
 has a relatively unknown program known as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Vine
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in which they send free items to a select group of people in exchange for
 reviews of the selected products.
 These individuals are required to submit a review online, whether it be
 positive or negative, on all the products they receive.
 Some experts claim that the reviews from Vine participants could be positively
 biased since they may be more inclined to leave encouraging feedback after
 receiving the product for free.
 Yet, Amazon claims that reviews from Vine participants are actually more
 negative, on average.
 Why does Amazon send out free products just to obtain a few extra reviews?
 According to the Amazon marketing department, even negatively reviewed
 items still sell better than items with no reviews.
 Thus, the Vine program is a possible solution to overcome the inherent
 costs of leaving a review online.
 Additional research and experiments are needed to verify this assumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Analysis Expectations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With my experiment, I can analyze both buyer and seller behavior.
 On the seller side, I hope to examine the impact of peer reviews on sellers
 quality selection and pricing behavior.
 Will there be any convergence to symmetric equilibria in terms of quality
 and price? How will the introduction of user reviews affect their decisions?
 Will sellers eventually start to converge to the same quality level? On
 the buyer's side, I hope to examine the impact of peer reviews on their
 buying decisions.
 Will reviewed goods, even those with a negative review, sell better than
 goods with no reviews? Will buyers even choose to pay a fee in order to
 leave a review?
\end_layout

